## Conventions, domain and reusable facts

Every number and variable is real unless an integer condition is explicit.
Define [x] as the greatest integer not exceeding x, NOT truncation toward zero.
Define {x}=x-[x]. Thus [x] <= x < [x]+1 and 0 <= {x} < 1.

For every integer n:

  [x]=n if and only if n <= x < n+1.
  The interval is [n,n+1[, with n included and n+1 excluded.

Proof: if n is the greatest integer <=x, then x cannot reach n+1; conversely,
if n<=x<n+1, n is an integer <=x and every greater integer is at least n+1>x.
This works without change for negative n.

Consequently the graph of y=[x] on [n,n+1[ is the horizontal segment y=n,
closed at (n,n) and open at (n+1,n). Its domain is R and range is Z.
The graph of y={x} on [n,n+1[ is y=x-n, closed at (n,0) and open at (n+1,1).
At the next integer n+1 its actual value is 0, not 1. Its domain is R and
range is [0,1[. These graph rules are derived from the definition, not read
from an unseen illustration.

Printed Cyrillic subpart labels are represented below by ordinal ASCII
a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h, preserving the source order.

