For a specified integer n the full family is x=n+t with 0<=t<1.
Then n<=n+t<n+1, proving [x]=n. Choose t=0,1/4,3/4, giving three
distinct examples for every printed subpart:

| Part | Required floor n | Complete family | Three valid examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| a | 14 | [14,15[ | 14, 14.25, 14.75 |
| b | 1 | [1,2[ | 1, 1.25, 1.75 |
| c | 0 | [0,1[ | 0, 0.25, 0.75 |
| d | -1 | [-1,0[ | -1, -0.75, -0.25 |
| e | -5 | [-5,-4[ | -5, -4.75, -4.25 |
| f | -10 | [-10,-9[ | -10, -9.75, -9.25 |

The family proof certifies each of the 18 examples individually because each
uses one of those three permitted t values. Negative examples stay between
n and n+1, not between n-1 and n. These are example responses, not unique
standard numbers: every real number in the corresponding interval is valid.

