## 662: evaluate six fractional parts and prove the general comparison

a. x=3.7; the requested order is {x}, {x+4}, {x-12}.

  x=3.7: 3<=3.7<4, so [x]=3 and {x}=3.7-3=0.7.
  x+4=7.7: 7<=7.7<8, so [x+4]=7 and {x+4}=7.7-7=0.7.
  x-12=-8.3: -9<=-8.3<-8, so [x-12]=-9 and
  {x-12}=-8.3-(-9)=0.7.

  All three results are 0.7, including the negatively shifted input.

b. x=-7.4; the requested order is {x}, {x-50}, {x+13}.

  x=-7.4: -8<=-7.4<-7, so [x]=-8 and {x}=-7.4-(-8)=0.6.
  x-50=-57.4: -58<=-57.4<-57, so [x-50]=-58 and
  {x-50}=-57.4-(-58)=0.6.
  x+13=5.6: 5<=5.6<6, so [x+13]=5 and {x+13}=5.6-5=0.6.

  All three results are 0.6, including the positively shifted input.

c. Source quantifiers: x is any real number and n is any integer.
   Put m=[x]. Then m<=x<m+1. Adding n gives
   m+n<=x+n<m+n+1. Since m+n is an integer, the floor characterization
   gives [x+n]=m+n=[x]+n. Therefore

     {x+n}=(x+n)-[x+n]=(x+n)-([x]+n)=x-[x]={x}.

   Conclusion: {x} and {x+n} are equal for every x in R and n in Z.
   The proof includes integer x, zero, negative x and negative n; neither
   strict positivity nor a noninteger x assumption was introduced.
   The integer restriction matters: for x=0 and n=1/2, the fractional
   parts would be 0 and 1/2, so the source claim must not be extended to
   arbitrary real shifts.

All six evaluations and the universally quantified proof are covered.

## 663: give several examples with each specified fractional part

For a prescribed r in [0,1[, the complete family is x=n+r, n in Z.
Indeed n<=n+r<n+1 implies [n+r]=n and {n+r}=n+r-n=r.
Conversely, if {x}=r then x=[x]+r, so x is in that family.

a. r=0.9: complete family {n+0.9 : n in Z}.
   Three examples and individual checks:
   0.9 has floor 0, hence fractional part 0.9-0=0.9;
   1.9 has floor 1, hence fractional part 1.9-1=0.9;
   -0.1 has floor -1, hence fractional part -0.1-(-1)=0.9.

b. r=0.02: complete family {n+0.02 : n in Z}.
   0.02 has floor 0, hence fractional part 0.02-0=0.02;
   3.02 has floor 3, hence fractional part 3.02-3=0.02;
   -0.98 has floor -1, hence fractional part -0.98-(-1)=0.02.

c. r=0: complete family Z, since {x}=0 iff x=[x] is an integer.
   0 has floor 0, hence 0-0=0;
   2 has floor 2, hence 2-2=0;
   -3 has floor -3, hence -3-(-3)=0.

Each subpart asks for examples, not a unique finite set. The families describe
all valid inputs and the nine explicit examples meet the request for several.

## 664: sketch both exponentials, then solve the equation and BOTH strict inequalities

Source task, complete: sketch y=0.7^x and y=0.4^x; use these two graphs to
solve 0.7^x=0.4^x, 0.7^x>0.4^x and 0.7^x<0.4^x.
There is no supplied graph or exercise image to inspect.

Let f(x)=(7/10)^x and g(x)=(2/5)^x. The bases are positive and below 1,
so both functions are defined for every real x, have range (0,+infinity),
and are continuous and strictly decreasing. For h>0,
f(x+h)/f(x)=(7/10)^h<1 and g(x+h)/g(x)=(2/5)^h<1,
which establishes the decreasing direction for each graph.

### Exact construction data

Use a shared coordinate system and common scales. Plot these exact points:

| x | f(x)=0.7^x | g(x)=0.4^x |
| --- | --- | --- |
| -2 | 100/49 (about 2.0408) | 25/4=6.25 |
| -1 | 10/7 (about 1.4286) | 5/2=2.5 |
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 7/10=0.7 | 2/5=0.4 |
| 2 | 49/100=0.49 | 4/25=0.16 |

The negative-x coordinates follow from reciprocals, not from negating
positive-x ordinates: (7/10)^(-1)=10/7, (7/10)^(-2)=100/49,
(2/5)^(-1)=5/2 and (2/5)^(-2)=25/4.

Draw two smooth decreasing curves through their points, labelled with their
function formulas. Both pass through the common y-intercept (0,1).
To the right they approach the horizontal asymptote y=0 from above without
ever touching it; to the left their values grow without bound. If drawing
through x=-2, the y scale must include g(-2)=6.25 rather than clip that point.
Do not stop the mathematical domain at the plotted table endpoints.
These are continuous exponential curves, not horizontal steps or a sawtooth.

For precise shape, a^x=exp(x ln a) has second derivative
(ln a)^2 a^x>0 for either base, so both curves bend upward everywhere.
This shape fact is supplementary; the source comparison can be read from
the relative curve heights and does not require a calculus response.

### Independently establish the relative heights and unique crossing

Because g(x)>0, divide without reversing inequalities:

  f(x)/g(x) = ((7/10)/(2/5))^x = (7/4)^x.

The ratio base 7/4 is greater than 1. Hence:

  x<0  implies (7/4)^x<1, so f(x)<g(x);
  x=0  implies (7/4)^x=1, so f(x)=g(x)=1;
  x>0  implies (7/4)^x>1, so f(x)>g(x).

There are no other crossings. Equivalently, equality gives
x ln(7/4)=0 and ln(7/4)>0, so x=0 only.
The graph of g lies above f to the left of the y-axis and below f to its
right. The lower base produces the larger value at negative exponents;
both curves being decreasing does not mean their mutual order is fixed.

### Graph-based solution of each requested relation

1. Equation 0.7^x=0.4^x: the curves share only (0,1), so the complete
   solution is x=0, the singleton {0}.
2. Strict inequality 0.7^x>0.4^x: the f curve is above the g curve exactly
   to the right of the intersection, so x>0, or (0,+infinity).
3. Strict inequality 0.7^x<0.4^x: the f curve is below the g curve exactly
   to the left of the intersection, so x<0, or (-infinity,0).

Zero is excluded from BOTH strict inequalities because the ordinates are
equal there. Sketch construction, equality and both strict inequalities
have all been solved. No recognition choice is being offered as evidence
that a learner can independently produce the sketch.

